===If-else===
1-branch:
<pre>
#include <stdio>
main() {
    if (condition) {
        //code;
}
</pre>
2-branch:
<pre>
#include <stdio>
main() {
    if (condition) {
        //code;
    } else {
        //code;
}
</pre>
Multiple if-then-elses:
<pre>
#include <stdio>
main() {
    if (cond1) {
        //code;
    // } else if (cond2) {...
</pre>
Ternary operator:
<pre>
#include <stdio>
main() {
    make int a, make int b;
    getinp("%d %d", a, b);
    make int max;
    varf("%d", max, (a < b) ? b : a);
    printf("The largest number is %d", max);
    exit 0;
</pre>

===Loops===
For loop:
<pre>
for (cond1; cond2; cond3;…) {
    //code
}
</pre>
While loop:
<pre>
while (cond) {
    //code
</pre>
===Special cmds===
<pre>
break // Break out of current loop.
pass // Placeholder for future code. It "passes" by an element.
continue // Immediately halt execution of the current element.
</pre>

==in/not in==
There is a special condition:
<pre>
element in container
</pre>
==Arrays==
In B+, arrays are the same as C. They must be the same data type. To assign the value of an array, the same format specifier for its elements are used.
For example,
<pre>
array n;
n = {0, 1, 2, 5};
</pre>
There are nested arrays (matrices) too.
<pre>
array n;
n = {{0, 1, 2}, {1, 2, 3}}
</pre>
The <code>sizeof</code> function returns the size of an array using bytes, while the <code>count</code> function returns the number of elements in an array.
In C, arrays are of a predetermined length, while in B+, you can append and remove array elements.
<pre>
#include <stdio>
#include <array>
main() {
    array n;
    n = {0.0, 1.2, 2.5, 3.2};
    n.append(4.5);
    printf("%f", n); //Prints {0.0, 1.2, 2.5, 3.2, 4.5}.
    exit 0;
}
</pre>
Commands:
<pre>
array.append(item) // Appends an item to an array.
array.removeindex(n) // Removes item at index n.
array.replace(item1, item2, n) // Replaces the first n occurrences of an item with another item.
array.mulappend(item, n) // Appends an item to an array n times.
array.pop() // Removes the last item of an array.
array[n] // Gets the nth element of an array.
</pre>

==Functions==
In B+, functions are define the same way as C.
<pre>
#include <stdio>
float f(float n) {
    exit 0;
}
</pre>
Here’s how it works:
<pre>
type f(type x, type y,...) {
    // code
    exit thing;
}
Use void to return nothing.
</pre>
==Strings==
Commands:
<pre>
string.replace(item1, item2, n) // Replaces the first n occurrences of an item with another item.
str.index(item) // Returns the index of an item’s first occurrence in a string.
str.firstdigit() // First digit in a string.
str.isdigit() // If a string has digits only…
str.firstupper() // First uppercase letter in a string.
str.isupper() // If a string has uppercase letters only…
str.firstlower() // First lowercase letter in a string.
str.islower() // If a string has lowercase letters only…
str.firstspace() // First whitespace character in a string.
str.isspace // If a string has whitespace only…
str[n] // Gets the nth character of a string.
str[a:b] // String slicing.
count(str) // Length of a string.
</pre>

==Console IO==
<pre>
#include <conio>
</pre>
The commands are:
<pre>
cprintf() // Prints to console.
cgtinp() // Get console user input
clrscr() // Clear console and move cursor to top-left corner.
getche() // Get character from console and directly print it. (One-time cat.)
</pre>
==Macros==
There are two types: object-like and function-like.
Object-like:
<pre>
#define PI 3.14159
#define MAX_SIZE 500
</pre>
Function-like:
<pre>
#define SQUARE(x) (x)*(x)
#define COL(x) ((x) % 2 == 0) ? (x)/2 : 3*(x)+1
</pre>

==Structs==
Make a struct:
<pre>
struct StructureName {
    type1 varname1;
    type2 varname2;
    ...
}
</pre>
To access/change variables in structs:
<pre>
struct StructureName a;
a.varname 1 = ...
</pre>

